When committing, git should open a text editor in which you
can put a commit message of any length. The usual way is to
put a summary first, then a longer description, like this:

| Change default characters to match commonly used options more closely
|
| Pilcrow for fountain is a bug relating to character set rendering.
| The character in question is /meant/ to be the top half of an
| integral. Also change boulder to 0, as diamonds are used for water
| in DECgraphics.

Without the "| " at the beginning, of course.
(This commit message is from the NetHack 4 variant, so it's
 not one I made up on the spot.)

The first line is used as a summary by convention; GitHub, which
you are using, respects this convention and will by default show
only this summary in commit logs etc. (though expanding to see
the full summary should be possible).

Commit messages with just a summary line can be fine too if
what you are committing is really that easily explained;
NH4 also has a lot of that kind of commits.

> As a side note, if anyone wants to know anything spoliery about
